  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1157 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe forecast period with a warm front draped across central and \nnortheastern Nebraska this evening and a shortwave trough sliding \nacross southeastern South Dakota and northwestern Iowa this evening. \nStrong to severe thunderstorms have developed across portions of \nSouth Dakota and Iowa, thus far, staying out of the OAX CWA. There \nis a small chance of a few isolated thunderstorms clipping the \nnortheastern and eastern portions of our area tonight as the low \nlevel jet kicks in. Models are in disagreement with some bringing in \nstorms along the front, while others have the storm potential in the \nvicinity of the trough. Fairly weak instability in place over our \narea will wane over the next 2-3 hours, so I went with more of a \nblend of the ECMWF and Canadian models, putting the best chances for \nstorms along our eastern CWA. \n\nOvernight, the warm front will lift north into northern Nebraska. \nThere is a small chance of some patchy fog in low-lying areas north \nof the front; however, most areas are not expected to have fog \ndevelop. Expect warmer temperatures Friday with highs reaching well \ninto the 80s. \n\nSaturday, brings a change in the weather pattern. An upper ridge \namplifies over the Great Lakes with the Great Plains coming under \nsouthwest flow with a tight pressure gradient in place. Expect \nbreezy to windy conditions beginning Friday night/Saturday morning \nand continuing through the day. South winds are expected to be \naround 10-20 mph for most areas with the strongest winds in \nnortheastern Nebraska. South winds will help bring warmer air into \nthe region, driving high temperatures into the mid to upper 80s. \nWinds gusting to around 30-40mph are expected. Minimum RH values \nwill fall into the 20-40% range. There is a concern for some fire \nweather conditions, particularly in northeastern Nebraska in areas \nthat were not affected by the recent heavy rain/flooding event. \n\nThe warm temperatures continue into Sunday, although they will be \nslightly cooler, topping out primarily in the mid-80s. Lows will be \nin the 60s. \n\nMonday into the extended forecast: A cold front slips through \neastern Nebraska/western Iowa on Monday, ushering in some cooler \nair. Highs will be in the 70s behind the front and the 80s ahead of \nit. By Tuesday and Wednesday, expect widespread 70s for highs. \nIsolated chances for showers/storms return to the forecast beginning \nMonday evening.",
